-- title:
--   Level one: ̃ j generates the modular function field
-- statement:
--   Let $k_0$ be a field. Inside the Laurent series field $k_0((q))$ consider the element $\tilde j =$ `jqModC k₀`, namely the product of the monomial $q^{-1}$ with the image in $k_0[[q]]$ of the integral power series `jNum` $= E_4^3\cdot$ `dedekindEtaUnitInv` under the coefficientwise map $\mathbb{Z}\to k_0$. Let $L =$ `modularFunctionFieldFullC k₀ 1` be the intermediate field of $k_0((q))/k_0$ generated by the set of all $q$-expansions `qExpand k₀ d` $(\tilde j)$ for nonzero $d$ dividing $1$. The element $\tilde j$ lies in $L$ (by `jqModC_mem_full k₀ 1`), so it defines a point of the subfield $L$. The assertion is that the intermediate field of $L/k_0$ generated by this single element is all of $L$; equivalently, $L$, viewed as an abstract extension of $k_0$ rather than as a subfield of $k_0((q))$, is the simple extension $k_0(\tilde j)$.
--
--   This records that at level $N = 1$ the modular function field is generated by the single $q$-expansion $\tilde j$, stated as a property of the abstract extension $L/k_0$ rather than of its embedding into $k_0((q))$. It is used in the form required by consumers that take "a single element generates $L$" as a hypothesis, in particular by [`ModularCurve.finrank_adjoin_aeval_jqModC`](thm.html#ModularCurve.finrank_adjoin_aeval_jqModC).
